{"ts": 1789717595.823516, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted features 20 (short), 35 (long) and 9 (short), with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.05 and a standard deviation of 0.35. This combination had the best in-sample Sharpe I found, 0.820, but that did not clear the search-adjusted bar of 1.022.\n\nI kept the prediction near zero because I found no sign of real signal in the panel:\n\n- **Single features:** I evaluated all 40 individually. Their Sharpes ranged from \u22120.54 to 0.49, a spread of roughly 0.22. That is about what pure noise gives over 5,000 days, where the standard error of a Sharpe is also about 0.22.\n- **Combinations:** I tried nine combinations built from the strongest singles. Each came in at about what adding independent noise Sharpes would predict. For example, the three strongest singles should combine to about 0.82, and that set measured 0.813. So the 0.820 of the submitted set is a selection effect, not an edge.\n- **Further search:** I stopped searching there. More evaluations would only have fitted the noise more closely.\n\nThe 0.35 covers out-of-sample sampling error and the small chance that there is some true signal. I do not know the length of the out-of-sample period, so that sampling error is only a rough allowance."}
